A||||14||BluByYou||"Floaty" handling at high speed
Z||000000||BluByYou||08-27-99||08:23 AM||||I posted a topic over the general forum that might be more appropriate here.  The question is:  At speeds over 100 mph my '87 GT feels like it is affected by wind lift.  the steeting gets very light and unprecise.  I've only done this 2 or 3 times, mainly because I don't need the ticket, but also because it strikes me as driving the car beyond it's safe envelope.  My car has 52,000 miles, new tires and has been well maintained.  Is this the handling others experience?  Up to 90 or so, it sticks like glue - very nice.
Z||000001||Dale||08-27-99||12:19 PM||||The good ol question of aerodynamics.  I would be willing to bet most people feal this.  The spring rate in the front end of the fiero is very soft which doesn't help.  But worse yet the nose, especialy on the coupe alows for alot of air to go underneath of the car creating lift.  A very dangerous thing at high speed.  Many cars had this problem, Datsun Z car, Jaguuar XKE, Delorean,Fiat X19,Etc. Etc.<br>You can eleveate this problem with good suspension and a good airdam which will redirect most of the air around the car instead of under the car.<br>If you look at most SCCA race cars you will notice the front is always lower than the back (body parts) with the back end fairly open.  This will create a vacuum under the car.  The faster the car goes the more the vacuum sucks the car down to the road.<p>I hope this helps explain a little bit.<p>Aloha
Z||000002||lowCG||08-27-99||01:21 PM||||Good point, I use late model Rabbit lowering springs('80-'81,Nuespeed#355.1001)that require a 2-1/4" (3"I.D.,4"O.D.) spacer.Also,I use '77 Cadilac Fleetwood,upper balljoints as lower units (welded to the top of the control arm,without the flange that they come in,opening towards the front of the car,and relieved slightly to allow maximum stud movement within the joint itself)in my Fiero,without the spring change,this alone will lower the frontend 1 5/8"(sorry Batboy,I'd told you it was only 5/8",oops).With Bilstein shocks,urethane inner swaybar bushings, this setup works very well to control the frontend.<br>I do all this myself,because you can't buy the setup ready made for the car.
Z||000003||Dale||08-27-99||02:22 PM||||lowcg<p>What did you use for the rear springs.  I will admitt it, I cheated and cut springs.  I have done this on many other cars without problems.  The set up on the 84 SE allows for substantial drop without messing up the geometry to much.  The car was cheap enough I didn't have anything to lose.
Z||000004||lowCG||08-27-99||07:19 PM||||I am using '86 Mazda 626 springs,the initial rate is firmer than the stock springs,but overall they're softer,lower the car about an inch,not using the stock upper spring cups,just seat on the plates in the towers,located by the heads of the allen head cap screws I use to mount the plates,also,I turn the upper bushing/plate upside down,and utilize the holes in the decklid that were put there(for some reason).<br>I may soon try out some stiffer ones from later version(heavier)of the same model.<br>The stock rear springs appear to be a great idea,however,the softer,initial portion is way too soft,and after moving rapidly through that part,you hit the 1-1/2 coils of doom(very stiff)at which point you begin to spin/slide.<br>Some put a large rear swaybar to try and get some response,but that will only make things worse.I will be using a 5/8" bar with my softer springs.<br>
Z||000005||theogre||08-27-99||07:30 PM||||aerolift is definatly a problem in these cars. Lowering it will help some, but you really must stop the air ramming under the car to have the best result.<p>There is a damn, according to some other web sites, for the GT front end. I have found no damns or skirts for either the 84-86 base nose, or the 87-88 base nose.
Z||000006||batboy||08-27-99||10:01 PM||||My 85 SE Batmobile's front end gets a little light at high speed too. I've been doing some serious shopping around for effective front air dams. IRM has a nice dam for the early Fiero standard wedge nose ($140, like what's on Sluppy's car). FOCOA carries one called DGP front air dam for coupes only ($125). They also have a stage 1 DGP kit that includes the air dam, rocker panels, and a cool wrap around rear spoiler that I might just buy.
Z||000007||Dale||08-28-99||01:29 PM||||Do you happen to have any pictures of these body parts.  I have been looking for a good front air dam and have come to the conclusion that I will have to fabricate one.<p>Mahalo
